# docs/ - the yamlover documentation book: a directory chapter (the examples/66 shape).
# Each toplevel chapter is a subdirectory with its own .yo/body.yo; this root body holds the
# intro prose and orders the chapters. Sources and migration progress: ../DOCSMIGRATION.md.
!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
yamlover
description: YAML with pointers – the language, its storage model, and its documents

- >
  **Yamlover** is a platform that supports the following use cases:

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - Cooperation and interoperability between humans, AI/LLMs, and automation tools
  - AI/LLM memory and context management
  - Simple text markup (called Marklower)
  - Personal note-taking, tagging, knowledge organization, mind management, and self-education
  - Knowledge graph storage and representation
  - Local-first data ownership and support for cloudless work
  - Controlled synchronization and collaboration over LAN or the internet

- >
  **Yamlover** stands for "YAML overlay", not "Yam lover". YAML is a concise, human-readable data
  structure language that has been around for years. We have modified and enhanced it, allowing it to
  overlay a variety of data formats.

- >
  The following are the major changes:

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - All YAML elements have been unified into a single, general-purpose type to represent arbitrary graph structures
  - Systematic pointer support has been added, enabling full support for directed, cyclic graphs
  - Yamlover can overlay filesystem directories, binary files, and other formats
  - Markup (Marklower) is integrated into the language

- >
  Yamlover still supports JSON, JSON5, and YAML. See the [full differences documentation](*::language:vs-yaml) here.

- >
  This book is the reference. It is itself a yamlover document tree—each page is a chapter,
  each paragraph is a marklower chunk, and the table of contents is the positional body you are
  now reading. The [language chapter](*::language) is the self-contained specification—principles,
  the abstract model, concrete representations, pointers, and queries. The [meta chapter](*::meta)
  is the meta language—yamlover's replacement for JSON Schema: typing, description, and
  presentation. The [transform chapter](*::transform) extends meta to conversions—transforms
  between yamlover shapes, typed like function prototypes. The [documents chapter](*::documents)
  explains the document layer built on top of yamlover—chapters, marklower prose, tables, and lists. The
  [supported foreign formats chapter](*::formats) lists everything else the tree can hold—Markdown,
  AsciiDoc, office documents, books, images, media, maps—and how far each integration goes. The
  [annotations chapter](*::annotations) covers how a user marks up a document—fragments and tags,
  where they live, and how they derive. The
  [demo server chapter](*::server) documents the web server that serves this book—the UI, projectional
  editors, and their state machines.
